{"builder":"Criteria the build must meet (the expect lines, word for word):\n- node bin/csv2json.js --tsv reads tab-separated input on stdin and prints the same JSON array that node bin/csv2json.js prints for the same table as CSV\n- README.md documents --tsv\n\nSteps:\n1. Read bin/csv2json.js, src/parse.js, test/csv.test.js and README.md. parse(text, sep = ',') in src/parse.js already takes a separator argument.\n2. In bin/csv2json.js, check whether process.argv.slice(2) contains '--tsv'. If it does, call parse(input, '\\t'); if not, keep calling parse(input) with the default comma, so output without the flag stays exactly as it is now.\n3. In test/csv.test.js, add a test that runs execFileSync('node', ['bin/csv2json.js', '--tsv'], { input: 'a\\tb\\n1\\t2\\n', encoding: 'utf8' }) and checks that the output equals the CSV CLI output for 'a,b\\n1,2\\n' ('[{\"a\":\"1\",\"b\":\"2\"}]\\n'). Use a table with at least two rows and three columns, including one empty field, and compare it with the CSV run of the same table.\n4. In README.md, document the --tsv flag: add a usage line such as `node bin/csv2json.js --tsv < table.tsv` and one sentence saying that the flag reads tab-separated input instead of CSV.\n5. Run npm test and make sure every test passes. Then open one pull request with these changes.","verifier":"Run every check at the PR head, from the repository root.\n\nCheck 1. Expect line: node bin/csv2json.js --tsv reads tab-separated input on stdin and prints the same JSON array that node bin/csv2json.js prints for the same table as CSV\nRun:\n  printf 'name\\tage\\tcity\\nann\\t30\\toslo\\nbob\\t\\trome\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js --tsv > /tmp/tsv.out; echo \"tsv exit $?\"\n  printf 'name,age,city\\nann,30,oslo\\nbob,,rome\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js > /tmp/csv.out; echo \"csv exit $?\"\n  cmp /tmp/tsv.out /tmp/csv.out && echo SAME; cat /tmp/tsv.out\nAlso run it with a single-row table: printf 'a\\tb\\n1\\t2\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js --tsv, then compare that with printf 'a,b\\n1,2\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js.\nPASS: both commands exit 0 in both cases, cmp prints SAME (the outputs are byte-identical), and the output is a JSON array of objects keyed by the header row (for the first table: [{\"name\":\"ann\",\"age\":\"30\",\"city\":\"oslo\"},{\"name\":\"bob\",\"age\":\"\",\"city\":\"rome\"}]).\nFAIL: either command exits non-zero, the outputs differ, or the --tsv output is not that JSON array (for example, a header key that still contains a tab).\n\nCheck 2. Expect line: README.md documents --tsv\nRun: grep -n -- '--tsv' README.md\nPASS: grep finds at least one line, and the text around it explains that --tsv makes the tool read tab-separated input.\nFAIL: grep finds nothing, or the matching lines do not describe what the flag does.\n\nAdvisory (not part of PASS or FAIL):\n- Run npm test and report any failure.\n- Check that the default CSV output is unchanged: printf 'a,b\\n1,2\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js should print [{\"a\":\"1\",\"b\":\"2\"}].\n- Report whether a test covering --tsv was added."}